{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfpoRuvzzvggPvDnySw79","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"No — that's stronger than what I'd defend, and the stronger version is actually wrong.\n\nThe claim I'd defend is narrower and it's about *provenance of the oracle*. Tests check the implementation against the oracle; they never check the oracle. So sufficiency turns entirely on where the oracle came from. When I write tests from an underdetermined spec and then write the implementation from the same reading, the oracle is my interpretation, and a green run can't validate it. That's a specific condition, not a property of test suites.\n\nFlip the condition and a passing suite is plenty. A pre-existing regression suite I didn't author is an independent oracle — passing it is real evidence. So are conformance vectors, golden files captured from production, a reference implementation to diff against, or characterization tests where the oracle is the old behavior rather than my reading. And for genuinely determined tasks — rename a symbol across the repo, bump a dependency — there's no interpretation gap for the suite to be blind to, so green is close to the whole story.\n\nI'd also resist \"never\" on practical grounds: a standard nothing can meet isn't a standard. If a passing suite never settles anything, \"done\" stops doing work, and I get license to append caveats forever while quietly pushing verification back onto you. That's the same disease as over-escalating — rigor-shaped, actually just risk transfer.\n\nFor slugify specifically, we're squarely in the bad case: I wrote those tests from an issue that underdetermines at least the non-ASCII behavior, so a green run will tell you the code matches my reading and nothing more. Your review of the test cases is the oracle here, which is why stopping at tests was the right call.\n\nOne correction to the obvious fix, though — property tests help less than they look. If I asserted `/^[a-z0-9]+(-[a-z0-9]+)*$/` and I'd misread \"non-alphanumeric,\" the property would encode the same misreading. Properties broaden coverage *under* a fixed interpretation; they don't validate the interpretation. Only an external oracle or a human does that."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4337,"cache_read_input_tokens":23408,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4337},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7b27ca5b-0ab9-4190-9ce8-1d290d2bfc9a","uuid":"94251398-5463-48f4-8ca6-ef384ea277b1","timestamp":"2026-10-08T12:52:48.493Z","request_id":"req_011CfpoRugcuagtaJcAoDTNm"}
